      "text": "To determine which of the five segments is the longest, we need to analyze the given angles and the properties of the segments in the figure. Let's label the vertices of the quadrilateral as \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\) in order, with \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) being the vertices opposite the common side \\(AC\\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\(\\angle BAC = 55^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle ACD = 55^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle BDC = 40^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle BCD = 75^\\circ\\)\n\nFirst, let's determine the measure of \\(\\angle ABC\\). Since the sum of the angles in a quadrilateral is \\(360^\\circ\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\angle ABC = 360^\\circ - \\angle BAC - \\angle ACD - \\angle BDC = 360^\\circ - 55^\\circ - 55^\\circ - 40^\\circ = 170^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, let's consider the segments. We need to compare the lengths of the sides \\(AB\\), \\(BC\\), \\(CD\\), \\(DA\\), and \\(DB\\). Since the angles involving these segments are given, we can use the Law of Sines in triangles to compare the lengths, but a more straightforward approach is to recognize that the length of a segment in a quadrilateral can be influenced by the angle subtended by it at the center of the quadrilateral.\n\nThe key observation is that the longest segment will be the one that subtends the largest angle at the center of the quadrilateral. The angles at the center of the quadrilateral are:\n\\[\n\\text{Angle at center} = 360^\\circ - \\angle ABC - \\angle BCD - \\angle BDC - \\angle ACD = 360^\\circ - 170^\\circ - 75^\\circ - 55^\\circ - 55^\\circ = 25^\\circ\n\\]\nThe segments that subtend angles of \\(180^\\circ\\) at the center will be the longest because they are opposite the largest angle of the quadrilateral.\n\nLet's examine the segments:\n- \\(AB\\) subtends \\(\\angle ACD = 55^\\circ\\)\n- \\(BC\\) subtends \\(\\angle BCD = 75^\\circ\\)\n- \\(CD\\) subtends \\(\\angle ADB = 180^\\circ - \\angle ACD - \\angle BCD = 180^\\circ - 55^\\circ - 75^\\circ = 50^\\circ\\)\n- \\(DA\\) subtends \\(\\angle BAC = 55^\\circ\\)\n- \\(DB\\) subtends \\(\\angle BDC = 40^\\circ\\)\n\nThe segment that subtends the largest angle of \\(180^\\circ\\) at the center is \\(AB\\), because \\(55^\\circ\\) is the largest angle in the angles we calculated for the quadrilateral.\n\nTherefore, the longest segment is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{AB}\n\\]",
