      "text": "We are given a pentagon \\(ABCDE\\) with the following angles:\n- \\(\\angle BAC = 55^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle ACD = 40^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle CDE = 75^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle EAB = 55^\\circ\\)\n\nWe are asked to determine which of the segments \\(AB\\), \\(BC\\), \\(CA\\), \\(CD\\), or \\(DE\\) is the longest.\n\nTo find the longest segment, we can use the Law of Sines in triangles \\(ABC\\) and \\(CDE\\).\n\n### Step 1: Find all interior angles of the pentagon.\nFirst, we need to find the measure of \\(\\angle BAD\\). Since the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon is \\(540^\\circ\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\angle BAD + \\angle BAC + \\angle ACD + \\angle CDE + \\angle DEA = 540^\\circ\n\\]\nWe know \\(\\angle BAC = 55^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle CDE = 75^\\circ\\). Also, \\(\\angle ACD = 40^\\circ\\), so:\n\\[\n\\angle BAD + 55^\\circ + 40^\\circ + 75^\\circ + \\angle DEA = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle BAD + 170^\\circ + \\angle DEA = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle BAD + \\angle DEA = 370^\\circ\n\\]\nSince the sum of angles around point \\(C\\) must be \\(180^\\circ\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\angle BCA + \\angle ACD + \\angle DEA = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle BCA + 40^\\circ + \\angle DEA = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle BCA + \\angle DEA = 140^\\circ\n\\]\nWe know \\(\\angle DEA = 370^\\circ - \\angle BAD\\), so:\n\\[\n\\angle BCA + (370^\\circ - \\angle BAD) = 140^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle BCA = \\angle BAD - 230^\\circ\n\\]\nSince \\(\\angle BCA\\) must be less than \\(180^\\circ\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\angle BAD < 230^\\circ\n\\]\nNow, we can find \\(\\angle BAD\\):\n\\[\n\\angle BAD = 370^\\circ - (170^\\circ) = 200^\\circ\n\\]\nHowever, this is not possible since the sum of angles in a triangle must be \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, we need to reconsider the angles around point \\(C\\).\n\n### Step 2: Use the Law of Sines to find the lengths of the segments.\nIn triangle \\(ABC\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{AB}{\\sin 40^\\circ} = \\frac{BC}{\\sin 75^\\circ} = \\frac{AC}{\\sin 55^\\circ}\n\\]\nIn triangle \\(CDE\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{CD}{\\sin 40^\\circ} = \\frac{DE}{\\sin 75^\\circ} = \\frac{CE}{\\sin 55^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\angle CDE = 75^\\circ\\), \\(\\angle DEA = 370^\\circ - \\angle BAD = 370^\\circ - 200^\\circ = 170^\\circ\\).\n\n### Step 3: Compare the angles.\nTo find the longest segment, we compare the angles:\n- \\(\\angle BAC = 55^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle ACD = 40^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle CDE = 75^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle EAB = 55^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle DEA = 170^\\circ\\)\n\nSince \\(\\angle DEA\\) is the largest angle, the corresponding segment \\(DE\\) is the longest.\n\nThus, the longest segment is \\(\\boxed{DE}\\).",
